       error->status = status;
       error->message = strdup(message);
       return error;
   }
   void free_error(Error* error) {
       free(error->message);
       free(error);
   }
   ```
   Here, you create a custom error structure that can hold both the status and a message. This approach allows you to manage memory manually, which can be useful in certain scenarios.

The choice of which method to use depends on the specific requirements of your application, including the complexity of the error conditions, the need for detailed error messages, and the level of control over memory management.

